    //
    // Instead of treating `&[Variant]` as a BLOCK SELECTOR (where one
    // variant's paragraphs survive and others are deleted), each
    // `&[Variant]` marker now just UPDATES the current variant for
    // subsequent `#[…]` clause lookups. Nothing gets deleted. IF/ELSE
    // structure stays intact and is processed normally by Step 1.
    //
    // This means a skeleton can look like:
    //   {{IF: type == Mutual}}
    //   &[Mutual NDA]
    //   1. Objective. #[Objective]    ← resolves to (Objective, Mutual NDA)
    //   {{ELSE}}
    //   &[NDA - One Way Out]
    //   1. Objective. #[Objective]    ← resolves to (Objective, NDA - One Way Out)
    //   {{ENDIF}}
    //
    // Both #[Objective] tokens are substituted with their respective
    // variant-specific clauses BEFORE Step 1 runs. Step 1 then drops
    // whichever IF/ELSE branch the user's DEF answers don't satisfy.
    //
    // Walk order matters: currentVariant changes at each `&[X]` marker
    // (in document order), and any `#[name]` token between this marker
    // and the next one uses X as its lookup variant.
    //
    // Starting variant (before any `&[]` marker) = OARS-detected type
    // if available, else null (clauses with no variant override fall
    // back to single-variant library entries).
    var variantsSeen = []; // for logging: [{variant, paraIdx}]
